.Dd November 8, 2026
.Dt DRM_SCHED_BATCH 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm drm_sched_batch
.Nd submit synchronous batches through the DRM scheduler
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In drm/drm_sched_batch.h
.Ft int
.Fn drm_sched_batch_queue_create "const char *name" "struct drm_sched_batch_queue **out"
.Ft int
.Fn drm_sched_batch_reserve "struct drm_sched_batch_queue *queue"
.Ft void
.Fn drm_sched_batch_unreserve "struct drm_sched_batch_queue *queue"
.Ft int
.Fn drm_sched_batch_prepare "struct drm_sched_batch *batch" "struct drm_file *file"
.Ft int
.Fn drm_sched_batch_capture "struct drm_sched_batch *batch"
.Ft int
.Fn drm_sched_batch_commit "struct drm_sched_batch *batch"
.Ft void
.Fn drm_sched_batch_cleanup "struct drm_sched_batch *batch"
.Ft void
.Fn drm_sched_batch_queue_stop "struct drm_sched_batch_queue *queue"
.Ft void
.Fn drm_sched_batch_queue_destroy "struct drm_sched_batch_queue *queue"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This native extension uses one entity and the existing DRM scheduler thread
for bounded synchronous batches.
It preserves the ordinary scheduler interface for other consumers.
The common layer owns admission, syncobj dependencies, publication, output
fences, cancellation and drain.
The driver owns hardware commands and resource retirement.
.Pp
Call
.Fn drm_sched_batch_size
to validate the driver allocation, reserve a slot, allocate zeroed storage and
initialize the embedded batch and strided items with
.Fn drm_sched_batch_init .
Use
.Fn drm_sched_batch_sync_alloc
to allocate normalized binary or timeline sync operations.
Each item identifies its contiguous atomic execution unit's first item.
Prepare retains syncobjs and creates output fences; capture retains every
input before commit can replace a binary payload.
All fallible driver preparation must also precede commit.
.Pp
Commit serializes scheduler initialization, publication and queue insertion.
Failure publishes nothing.
Success transfers ownership to the scheduler; the caller must no longer
access the batch.
On rejection the caller cleans up, releases driver resources and unreserves.
For accepted work the sleeping release callback cleans up and frees driver
storage, then the common layer unreserves.
Callbacks run outside scheduler and fence locks.
.Pp
The worker waits for actual completion and propagates dependency errors,
including nested fence-chain payload errors.
It executes each atomic unit once and completes its outputs.
Failure completes that unit and remaining batch outputs with the error.
A driver callback may identify exact intra-unit dependencies handled by
hardware.
Automatic timeout replay is disabled.
Execute callbacks return only after retirement or quarantine and must release
driver gates before returning.
An error fence does not establish that DMA stopped.
.Pp
Policy bounds each queue to eight reserved batches, each batch to 256 items
and 1 MiB charged memory, and producer searches to 1048576 steps per batch.
Each item's publication and completion waits have a ten-second deadline.
.Fn drm_sched_batch_deadline
supplies the same deadline policy for driver resource barriers.
Output fences have independent contexts and can outlive the queue.
.Pp
Stop closes admission and cancels work at its next cancellation probe.
The owner must keep the queue live throughout API entry.
Before destroy, exclude new calls and quiesce entrants not yet in reserve.
Destroy waits for registered admission waiters, preparation and sleeping
retirement, then destroys the empty entity and joins the scheduler thread.
It must run outside that thread, without driver execution gates.
The queue name remains live through destruction.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions returning int return zero on success or a positive BSD errno.
Admission failure leaves outputs unpublished.
Accepted output fences report negative errors on execution failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dma_fence_chain_get_payload_status 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
interface first appeared in EmberBSD.
